<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  HARD SHIPPING GATE — see 02-native-sdk-overview.md §5.1.

  Since 1 May 2024, an SDK using a required-reason API must declare it in a privacy
  manifest or App Store Connect rejects the upload with ITMS-91053 (Missing API
  declaration). That rejection lands on the MERCHANT's upload, not ours: shipping this
  file wrong or absent breaks every integrator's release, which is the worst possible
  first impression. This file is therefore correct from day one, before any SDK
  behaviour exists to need it.

  Known failure mode to validate before release (03-implementation-strategy.md §3.1):
  AppsFlyer's manifest failed to bundle correctly in the *static* SPM variant (their
  issue #281). Propagation must be checked against a real consumer app, not just a
  local `swift build`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      No tracking as defined by App Tracking Transparency. The ATT question itself is
      02 §12 open question 1 and needs a legal decision before iOS ships; if that
      decision changes, this key and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains change with it.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <!--
      TODO(02 §5.1): declare data collection accurately once the identity module
      lands. The anonymous id is collected and linked to a user identity, so this
      array will need an NSPrivacyCollectedDataType entry. Empty today because the
      SDK is scaffolding and collects nothing — declared rather than omitted so the
      shape is already here.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <!--
      UserDefaults is one of the five required-reason categories, and this SDK stores
      the anonymous id there (02 §4, and the storage row of the §5 dependency table).
      Declared ahead of the code that uses it: an under-declared manifest is a
      merchant-facing rejection, an over-declared one is not.

      CA92.1 = access scoped to the app itself. Verify against Apple's live reason
      list before release — 02 §5.1 notes C56D.1 (SDK reads/writes only its own
      namespace) may be the better fit and is what many third-party SDKs ship.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
